The NoJIT interpreter keeps the IR of each block it ran (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0022)

With 0019, a block missing from the AOT image went through the frontend,
the IR passes and a copy into a new InterpreterIR on every visit, and a
loop in such code paid for all of it on every iteration: ExitFunctionLink
does not link to an interpreted block, so each visit comes back to
CompileBlock.

CompileBlock now keeps that IR in ContextImpl::InterpCache by guest entry
and runs a block it has seen before from there. The IR also records the
guest pages the block was decoded from (the FrontendDecoder's CodePages),
and InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange, which every invalidation goes through
(a write to a code page, a protection change, a free or unmap, an untracked
write), drops each cached block with a page in the range, as it drops
compiled blocks. When a block's IR is cached, its pages are marked as
executable ranges (MarkGuestExecutableRange), as a compiled block's are,
so a write to an RWX page traps and invalidates. The cache is read and
filled under the shared CodeInvalidationMutex, which invalidation takes
uniquely, and a block runs from its own reference to the IR, so it can be
dropped while it runs. A delivery compile (ml455, no invalidation lock
held) neither reads nor fills the cache, and custom IR is never cached.

The summary at NtTerminateProcess now reads "[interp] fallback ran N
blocks (M distinct) that are not in the AOT image, H of them from the IR
cache (B IR builds, D dropped by invalidation)".

